        "bodyFile": "/workspace/task-evidence/content-basis-calibration-02/origin-fetches/30b10ae9dbe425c0.body",
        "title": "Does the Earth's Magnetic Field Serve as a Reference for Alignment of the Honeybee Waggle Dance? | PLOS One",
        "textFile": "/workspace/task-evidence/content-basis-calibration-02/origin-fetches/30b10ae9dbe425c0.text.txt",
        "textSHA256": "fbdabe01297602d563ebd53f8349a942caf71be6e8e5ed20e973882db6a97741"
      },
      "title": "Does the Earth's Magnetic Field Serve as a Reference for Alignment of the Honeybee Waggle Dance?",
      "publishedAt": "2014-12-26",
      "dateNotes": "Article publication date appears in source.",
      "passage": "We tested the effect of the LGMF on the recruitment success of forager bees by placing observation hives inside large Helmholtz coils, and then either reducing the LGMF to 2% or shifting its apparent declination. Neither of these treatments reduced the number of nest mates that waggle dancing forager bees recruited to a feeding station located 200 m north of the hive. These results indicate that the LGMF does not act as the reference for the alignment of waggle-dancing bees.",
      "passageSHA256": "e11584d710c82f97afd1cb1c605210ef446a3c1912a3d21366818220cb2837d8",
      "extraction": "Contiguous whitespace-normalized visible HTML text.",
      "qualifyingRecordCount": 1
    },
